
DHCP
DHCP is a network protocol used to automate the process of assigning IP address and other configuration details to devices in a network. Without DHCP, each device that is connecting to a network would be done manually by setting the IP address configuation which can be time-consuming and error-prone. DHCP also assigns with Domain Name System (DNS) addresses,subnet masks and default gateway, enabling devices to communicate with the internet and other devices within the network. The DHCP port number for the server is 67 and for the client is 68. It is a client-server protocol that uses UDP services. An IP address is assigned from a pool of addresses for each device connected to the network.
How DHCP works ?
DHCP protocol works in a 4 step process:
DHCP Discover: This is the first message sent by a DHCP client to check whether any DHCP server is available on the network and to start getting an IP address. When a device (client) connects to the network, it doesn’t have an IP address.
It sends a DHCP Discover message as a broadcast to find available DHCP servers.
DHCP Offer:The DHCP server receives the discover message. DHCP server replies with an offer that includes an available IP address and configuration options (like subnet mask, gateway, DNS, and lease time).
DHCP Request: DHCP Request is the message where the client confirms one chosen offer and asks the selected DHCP server to allocate that IP officially. It also tells other DHCP servers not to keep their offered IP reserved. The client chooses one of the offers (if multiple servers respond).
DHCP Acknowledgement: DHCP ACK is the final confirmation sent by the selected DHCP server after it receives the client’s DHCP Request. This message officially assigns the IP address to the client and provides the full set of network configuration parameters.
DHCP Component
DHCP Server: A dedicated device, router or software device that manages the database of IP addresses, configuration and lease durations.
DHCP Client: A network connected device that requests IP configuration from the Server.
DHCP Relay Agent: A router/host configured to listen for and forward DHCP broadcast messages between clients and server across different subnets.
